{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 argues the expert will outperform the panel on tasks requiring latent causal reasoning, but it never addresses the required evidence-discrimination step of the problem: what evidence within a single evaluation would distinguish a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that tracks the right answer. Its counterconditions describe boundary cases that would weaken its claim, but none of them identify a single-evaluation diagnostic for genuine vs coincidental reliability. By arguing only for expert superiority on a task subset, it leaves the central question—how to tell, in one evaluation, whether panel performance is substantive or coincidental—unanswered. The target's scope is therefore too narrow for the problem as stated.","target":"55e7b4f1b47f2aa59b6339d5ef6a4710dde9c87af53fa01d148bb3019fe4a223"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 proposes distinguishing genuine from coincidental panel reliability by whether panel errors are structured (genuine causal model) vs random (mere correlational filter). But this diagnostic is unsound for the problem's requirement: a correlational checklist that systematically tracks the right answer can produce structured errors that mirror the task's structure, because surface regularities often correlate with causal dependencies. Conversely, a genuine causal-model checklist can produce random-looking errors if it handles most causal links correctly and fails on rare independent cases. Thus error structure within a single evaluation cannot reliably map to causal vs correlational status as the target claims, so it fails to provide a valid single-evaluation distinguishing criterion.","counterexample":[{"checklist_type":"correlational","description":"A checklist that uses a surface symptom correlated with a disease to classify cases; failures concentrate on atypical presentations where the symptom misleads, yielding structured errors despite no causal model.","error_pattern":"structured","true_status":"coincidental"},{"checklist_type":"causal","description":"A checklist encoding the correct causal chain for a fault diagnosis but failing on rare independent fault modes not in the model; residual errors are sparse and appear random across evaluation instances.","error_pattern":"random","true_status":"genuine"}],"target":"ccc8278a27b1aa996f964d5c97eeef5fffdbfb17505f30bfff8010b2dfde72b5"}]}